<map version="freeplane 1.9.0">
<node TEXT="My Theology">
<node TEXT="Scripture" POSITION="right" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Inerrancy  [T1 · certain · study]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Scripture is without error in all that it affirms.</p><p>WHY: Christ&#x27;s own use of the Old Testament, and Scripture&#x27;s self-attestation.</p><p>NOT: Limited infallibility — trustworthy on faith and salvation, errant on history and science.</p><p>STUDY: Work through the Chicago Statement&#x27;s nuances before settling on the precise formulation.</p><p>TEXTS: 2 Tim 3:16-17; John 10:35</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Canon  [T1 · confident]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Sixty-six books. The church recognised the canon; it did not create it.</p><p>NOT: The Roman Catholic deuterocanon as Scripture.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Sufficiency of Scripture  [T1 · confident]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Scripture is sufficient for faith and practice. No new revelation adds to it.</p><p>WHY: The necessary counterweight to continuationism — this is why prophecy can never bind the conscience.</p><p>TEXTS: 2 Tim 3:16-17; Heb 1:1-2; Rev 22:18-19</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Clarity of Scripture  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The main things are the plain things. Scripture&#x27;s central message is accessible to ordinary readers. Use clear teachings to understand unclear teachings</p><p>NOT: Teaching that requires an anointed interpreter to unlock the text.</p><p>TEXTS: Ps 119:105; Ps 19:7-8</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Hermeneutic method  [T2 · confident · study]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Authorial intent, genre and context govern meaning; application follows meaning.</p><p>NOT: Readings that detach a text from its context.</p><p>STUDY: Look into different explicit approaches / methods (e.g. Grammatical-historical, allegorical, &quot;rhema&quot;)</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Translations  [T3 · confident · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Formal and dynamic translations are both serviceable. No version is uniquely authorised. Translations should be used considerate of where they sit in word-for-word vs paraphrase (e.g. paraphrase not for deep study)</p><p>NOT: KJV-onlyism.</p><p>STUDY: Investigate the different streams of translations for ancestry.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="God" POSITION="left" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Trinity  [T1 · certain]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: One God in three co-equal, co-eternal persons.</p><p>WHY: The creeds settle this. To deny it is to deny the faith.</p><p>TEXTS: Matt 28:19; John 1:1; 2 Cor 13:13</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Eternal generation of the Son  [T1.5 · confident]" COLOR="#8a4a24" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The Son is eternally begotten, not made. Nicene language affirmed as written.</p><p>WHY: Follows directly from holding the creeds at tier one.</p><p>TEXTS: John 1:14; John 1:18</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="EFS / ESS  [T2.5 · open · study]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Undecided.</p><p>STUDY: Grudem and Ware hold that the Son eternally submits to the Father&#x27;s authority. Critics say this gives the persons distinct wills, which is a Nicene problem — a will belongs to a nature. Decide, because my case for male headship is authority-based and EFS is the usual way that argument is made.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Cor 11:3; John 5:19</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Classical theism  [T2 · open · study]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Sympathetic to divine simplicity, immutability, impassibility and eternity — not yet settled.</p><p>STUDY: Work through simplicity and impassibility properly. Ortlund is a good entry point.</p><p>TEXTS: Mal 3:6; James 1:17</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="God&#x27;s relation to time  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Timeless eternity, or temporal since creation? Craig&#x27;s temporal-since-creation view pairs naturally with Molinism.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Open theism  [T1.5 · rejected]" COLOR="#8a4a24" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Rejected. God&#x27;s knowledge of the future is exhaustive.</p><p>WHY: God declares the end from the beginning and knows a word before it is on the tongue. The rejection rests on those texts directly, not on any particular account of how exhaustive knowledge coheres with creaturely freedom.</p><p>TEXTS: Isa 46:9-10; Ps 139:4</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Divine foreknowledge  [T3 · leaning]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Middle knowledge (Molinism) — God knows what free creatures would freely do in any circumstance.</p><p>WHY: The best available account of how exhaustive foreknowledge and genuine freedom fit together. Held as an explanation, not as a test of orthodoxy — that God foreknows exhaustively is settled at a far higher tier than how he does.</p><p>STUDY: The grounding objection is the real cost: what makes the counterfactuals of creaturely freedom true prior to any divine decree? Worth understanding before leaning harder.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Sam 23:10-13; Matt 11:21-23</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Christ" POSITION="right" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Deity and humanity  [T1 · certain]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Fully God and fully man. Two natures in one person, without confusion or division (Chalcedon).</p><p>TEXTS: John 1:1,14; Col 2:9</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Virgin birth  [T1 · certain]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Affirm as historical.</p><p>TEXTS: Matt 1:23; Luke 1:34-35</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Bodily resurrection  [T1 · certain]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Christ rose bodily and in space and time. Without it there is no Christianity (1 Cor 15).</p><p>TEXTS: Luke 24:39; John 20:27</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Christ&#x27;s use of divine power  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Voluntary restraint (krypsis). He retained every divine attribute in full, and freely chose not to exercise them independently of the Father and the Spirit.</p><p>WHY: A very strong man can choose to use less force in an arm wrestle without ceasing to be strong.</p><p>NOT: Ontological kenosis — that he divested himself of divine attributes in the incarnation. Krypsis is the historic and safer position; don&#x27;t let it get labelled kenoticism.</p><p>TEXTS: Phil 2:6-7; Mark 13:32</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Impeccability  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Christ could not have sinned, though his temptations were genuine.</p><p>TEXTS: Heb 4:15; James 1:13</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Atonement  [T1.5 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#8a4a24" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Likely penal substitution as the centre, with Christus Victor and ransom as real complementary facets.</p><p>STUDY: Test penal substitution against the governmental view — that is the classic Arminian alternative.</p><p>TEXTS: Isa 53:5-6; Rom 3:25; Col 2:15</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Extent of the atonement  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Universal provision. Christ died sufficiently for all; the benefit is applied on faith (Analogy: A girlfriend rejecting a proposal does not mean the love and the offer is not real/present).</p><p>WHY: Follows from a non-Calvinist soteriology.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 John 2:2; 1 Tim 2:6</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Holy Spirit" POSITION="left" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Baptism in the Holy Spirit  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: A distinct empowering subsequent to conversion. Tongues is usual but not the necessary initial evidence.</p><p>WHY: There are many subsequent infillings, and the first one will be a clear distinctive.</p><p>NOT: The classic Pentecostal initial-physical-evidence doctrine (INC, Assemblies of God).</p><p>TEXTS: Acts 1:8; Acts 19:1-6</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Sealing of the Spirit  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The sealing (Eph 1:13) is distinct from both baptism and infilling. It is God&#x27;s mark of ownership and his guarantee, given to every believer at conversion.</p><p>WHY: This is what keeps the subsequence doctrine from implying that some Christians lack the Spirit.</p><p>TEXTS: Eph 1:13; 2 Cor 1:22</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Continuationism  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: All the gifts continue. None were withdrawn with the apostles.</p><p>NOT: Cessationism.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Cor 12:4-11; 1 Cor 14:39</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Prophecy  [T2 · open · study]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Provisionally fallible and to be weighed (Grudem) — held loosely.</p><p>STUDY: The word &quot;prophecy&quot; is used to mean several quite different things, both in the text and in practice. Map the semantic range first; the doctrine will follow from it.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Cor 14:29; 1 Thess 5:20-21</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Tongues  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Both a private prayer language and a congregational gift. Congregational use requires interpretation and order (1 Cor 14). Congregational can  be in the form of other earthly languages or prophesy which reveals situations / inner secrets to bring encouragement/conviction in prophetic edification of the church.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Cor 14:2; 1 Cor 14:27-28</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Apostles and prophets today  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The functions continue — church planting, sending, prophetic ministry. The offices carrying governing authority do not.</p><p>NOT: NAR-style governmental apostles and prophets.</p><p>TEXTS: Eph 4:11-13</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Healing  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Secured in the atonement, but already and not yet. God genuinely heals now; full healing awaits the resurrection.</p><p>WHY: Unanswered prayer is therefore not necessarily a failure of faith.</p><p>NOT: Word of Faith guarantees.</p><p>TEXTS: Isa 53:4-5; James 5:14-15; 2 Cor 12:8-9</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Manifestations  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: God may act physically and visibly on people, but no manifestation validates a ministry. Test by fruit, character and doctrine, never by phenomena.</p><p>TEXTS: Matt 7:15-20; 1 John 4:1</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Fruit from Bad Ministers  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Working of the Holy Spirit never validates a minister but God may minister through people DESPITE their fruit, character and doctrine.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Humanity and sin" POSITION="right" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Image of God  [T1.5 · confident · study]" COLOR="#8a4a24" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: All humans bear God&#x27;s image inherently and equally. It grounds human dignity and is not lost in the fall.</p><p>STUDY: The views across time of the nature of the imago dei.</p><p>TEXTS: Gen 1:26-27; Gen 9:6</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Historical Adam  [T2.5 · open · study]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Rom 5 and 1 Cor 15 appear to require a real Adam whose act brought sin and death. Decide whether Genesis 2–3&#x27;s genre changes that.</p><p>TEXTS: Rom 5:12-19; 1 Cor 15:21-22</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Original sin  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Inherited corruption only (Wesleyan), or inherited guilt as well (Augustinian)? Arminian instincts point toward the former.</p><p>TEXTS: Ps 51:5; Ezek 18:20</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Depravity and prevenient grace  [T2.5 · open · study]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: If grace is resistible and the human response genuinely free, what enables that response? Prevenient grace, or an unaided free will?</p><p>TEXTS: John 6:44; Titus 2:11</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Dichotomy vs trichotomy  [T3 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Dichotomy. A human being is body and one immaterial self; &quot;spirit&quot; and &quot;soul&quot; name that self from different angles rather than two separable parts.</p><p>WHY: Scripture uses the two words interchangeably — the same act is ascribed to soul and to spirit, and death is described as the departure of either. The trichotomist texts read more naturally as emphatic wholeness than as an inventory of parts.</p><p>NOT: Trichotomy — body, soul and spirit as three distinct constituents, with the human spirit as the organ of contact with God.</p><p>STUDY: Work through 1 Thess 5:23 and Heb 4:12 properly rather than resting on the interchangeability argument alone.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Thess 5:23; Heb 4:12</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Age of accountability  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Those who die before moral responsibility are saved through Christ&#x27;s work.</p><p>TEXTS: 2 Sam 12:22-23; Deut 1:39</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Salvation" POSITION="left" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Sovereignty and free will  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: There is no genuine conflict between them.</p><p>WHY: A husband and wife choose each other, and neither choice overrides the other&#x27;s freedom. Molinism is the most likely framework for spelling this out, but the compatibility is held on the texts and does not stand or fall with it.</p><p>NOT: The Calvinist framing that makes these a dilemma in the first place.</p><p>TEXTS: Phil 2:12-13; Josh 24:15</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Election  [T2.5 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Not unconditional in the Calvinist sense. God&#x27;s choosing and the free human response are compatible.</p><p>STUDY: With Molinism at T3, conditional election stands on classical Arminian grounds and a Molinist account becomes an optional refinement rather than a fork to choose. Confirm that is the settled shape.</p><p>TEXTS: Rom 8:29; Eph 1:4-5</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Perseverance and apostasy  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Can a genuine believer be lost? Heb 6, Heb 10 and John 15 against 1 John 2:19 and John 10:28.</p><p>TEXTS: Heb 6:4-6; John 10:28-29</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Assurance  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Follows from perseverance. Aim for a doctrine that gives real assurance without rendering the warning passages hypothetical.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 John 5:13; Rom 8:16</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Lordship salvation  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Saving faith necessarily produces fruit. A faith that changes nothing was never faith. Works are evidence, never ground.</p><p>TEXTS: James 2:17; Matt 7:21-23</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Justification  [T1 · confident]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: By grace through faith, on the ground of Christ&#x27;s work alone.</p><p>NOT: The New Perspective&#x27;s reduction of justification to covenant membership.</p><p>TEXTS: Eph 2:8-9; Rom 5:1</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Regeneration and baptism  [T2.5 · leaning]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Baptism is genuinely instrumental rather than a bare symbol — but it is not the moment of regeneration.</p><p>TEXTS: Titus 3:5; 1 Pet 3:21</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Church" POSITION="right" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Women in ministry  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Soft complementarian. Women may preach, teach, lead ministries and be ordained to most roles; the senior pastor and elder office is male.</p><p>WHY: A male should carry responsibility for the discipleship of the adult men of the church. It is a difference in authority, not a difference in value.</p><p>NOT: Both egalitarianism and the hard complementarianism (that restricts women&#x27;s teaching generally).</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Tim 2:12; Gal 3:28</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Church government  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Plural eldership with a senior pastor leading.</p><p>NOT: A single unaccountable leader; and congregational vote as final authority.</p><p>TEXTS: Acts 14:23; 1 Tim 5:17</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Translocal oversight  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Network and apostolic relationships are valuable for accountability and sending, but do not hold governing authority over the local eldership.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Baptism  [T2 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Believers only, by immersion, and genuinely instrumental — a means of grace, not merely a public symbol.</p><p>STUDY: What does a &quot;means of grace&quot; actually do? Resolve this alongside the Lord&#x27;s Supper; they are one question.</p><p>TEXTS: Acts 2:38; 1 Pet 3:21</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="The Lord&#x27;s Supper  [T2.5 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Likely spiritual real presence (Calvin) — Christ genuinely communicated to believers by the Spirit.</p><p>NOT: Bare memorialism, which is too thin for 1 Cor 10:16; and transubstantiation.</p><p>STUDY: Same study as baptism. Also study Calvins full definition.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Cor 10:16; 1 Cor 11:23-26</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Membership and discipline  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Meaningful membership and Matthew 18 discipline are normal church life, aimed at restoration rather than punishment.</p><p>TEXTS: Matt 18:15-17; 1 Cor 5:4-5</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Tithing and giving  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The tithe is a wise baseline, not binding law. Giving should be generous, cheerful and sacrificial.</p><p>NOT: Teaching the tithe as an obligation on Christians.</p><p>TEXTS: Mal 3:10; 2 Cor 9:7</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Worship  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Style/expression is a matter of liberty. Content, truthfulness and edification are not.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Last things" POSITION="left" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Second coming  [T1 · certain]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Christ will return bodily, visibly and personally.</p><p>TEXTS: Acts 1:11; 1 Thess 4:16</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Millennium  [T3 · leaning]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Premillennial.</p><p>WHY: Held as a soft lean, with genuine appreciation for much of the amillennial argument.</p><p>TEXTS: Rev 20:1-6</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Historic vs dispensational premillennialism  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Historic premillennialism. Christ returns before the millennium, but without dispensationalism&#x27;s separate activities for Israel and the church.</p><p>WHY: Follows from progressive covenantalism. Worth stating explicitly, because &quot;premil&quot; in a Pentecostal setting is normally assumed to mean the dispensational version.</p><p>STUDY: I want to study further on the relation with Israel.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Israel and the church  [T3 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Progressive covenantalism — the church fulfils the promises in Christ without simply replacing Israel, and Rom 11 keeps a real future for ethnic Israel.</p><p>NOT: Both dispensationalism and strict replacement theology.</p><p>STUDY: Keen to study further. This node largely determines the millennium and rapture nodes.</p><p>TEXTS: Rom 11:17-24; Rom 11:25-26</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Rapture  [T4 · open]" COLOR="#33526e" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Post-tribulation or mid-tribulation. Not pre-tribulation.</p><p>WHY: A pre-tribulation rapture depends on dispensational machinery not held.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Thess 4:16-17; 2 Thess 2:3</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Hell  [T3 · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Eternal conscious torment.</p><p>STUDY: Give conditional immortality an honest hearing rather than a dismissal. The exegetical case (Fudge; Stott&#x27;s hesitation) is serious even if it is finally wrong.</p><p>TEXTS: Matt 25:46; Rev 20:10; Matt 10:28</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Intermediate state  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The dead in Christ are consciously with him, awaiting the resurrection of the body.</p><p>WHY: Guards against the popular error that a disembodied heaven is the final hope.</p><p>TEXTS: 2 Cor 5:8; Phil 1:23</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="New creation  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The hope is a renewed, physical heaven and earth — not an escape from matter.</p><p>TEXTS: Rev 21:1-4; Rom 8:19-21</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Creation and science" POSITION="right" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Creation ex nihilo and design  [T1.5 · certain]" COLOR="#8a4a24" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: God created deliberately, out of nothing, with purpose.</p><p>TEXTS: Gen 1:1; Heb 11:3</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Age of the earth  [T3 · leaning]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Old earth — roughly seventy-thirty.</p><p>WHY: Young-earth arguments are respected as genuinely possible, not dismissed.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Genre of Genesis 1  [T3]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: True theology told in ancient literary form. Not a scientific chronology, and so not in competition with one.</p><p>STUDY: Further study into &#x27;mytho history&#x27; genre.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Evolution  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Depends on the historical Adam question. Resolve that first, then this.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="The flood  [T3 · open]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: A real judgement event. Global or regional left open.</p><p>TEXTS: Gen 7:11-24; 2 Pet 3:5-6</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Miracles and science  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: God acts in the world he made.</p><p>TEXTS: John 5:17; Col 1:17</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="The unseen realm" POSITION="left" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Angels and demons  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Real, personal, created spiritual beings. Neither mythology nor metaphor.</p><p>TEXTS: Heb 1:14; Eph 6:12</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Divine council  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Heiser&#x27;s reading of Psalm 82, Deut 32:8-9 and Genesis 6. Interested, not yet convinced. Assess the ancient Near Eastern background case on its merits.</p><p>TEXTS: Ps 82:1; Deut 32:8-9</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Genesis 6 sons of God  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Angelic view or Sethite line. Rides on the divine council question.</p><p>TEXTS: Gen 6:1-4; Jude 1:6</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Can a Christian be demonised  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: &quot;Demonised&quot; is the better word than possessed or oppressed. A believer can come under real demonic influence in mind, will and body without being owned, because influence admits of degrees in a way ownership does not.</p><p>WHY: Deliverance needs a doctrine of degree, not a doctrine of parts. Stating it this way keeps the pastoral practice intact whether a person is dichotomist or trichotomist.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Deliverance ministry  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Legitimate, and integrated with repentance, discipleship and where needed medical care — never a substitute for them.</p><p>TEXTS: Mark 16:17; Acts 16:18</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Territorial spirits  [T4 · open]" COLOR="#33526e" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Spiritual powers over regions is a plausible biblical category. Strategic-level spiritual warfare methodology is not warranted by it.</p><p>TEXTS: Dan 10:13; Rev 2:13</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Ethics" POSITION="right" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Marriage and sexuality  [T2 · certain]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Marriage is between one man and one woman. Same-sex sexual activity is sin. Held with warmth, not hostility.</p><p>WHY: Same-sex attraction is temptation, in the same category as temptation toward drunkenness, greed or idolatry. The temptation is not itself the sin.</p><p>NOT: Affirming readings; and equally the hostility that treats this sin as uniquely disqualifying.</p><p>TEXTS: Gen 2:24; Rom 1:26-27</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Gender identity  [T2 · certain]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Embodiment is a given good and identity is not self-created, so transition is not the answer. Gender dysphoria is real suffering rather than rebellion, and deserves compassion.</p><p>WHY: Held due to genuine concern about medicalisation of minors, social contagion and institutional capture.</p><p>TEXTS: Gen 1:27; Ps 139:13-14</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Divorce and remarriage  [T3 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Adultery, desertion, and abuse named explicitly as its own ground rather than squeezed under desertion.</p><p>STUDY: Low confidence. Work the Matthean exception and 1 Cor 7 properly.</p><p>TEXTS: Matt 19:9; 1 Cor 7:15</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Abortion  [T2 · certain]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The unborn bear God&#x27;s image from conception. Abortion takes a human life.</p><p>TEXTS: Ps 139:13-16; Jer 1:5</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="IVF and embryos  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: If personhood begins at conception, then embryo creation, freezing and discard need an answer.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Euthanasia  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Deliberately ending a life is not care. Suffering is met with palliative care and presence.</p><p>TEXTS: Exod 20:13; Job 14:5</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Prosperity teaching  [T1.5 · certain]" COLOR="#8a4a24" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Rejected as a serious distortion of the gospel.</p><p>NOT: Guaranteed-return giving; health and wealth as covenant right.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Tim 6:5-10</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Alcohol  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Drunkenness is sin. Abstinence is wisdom for some and not a law for all.</p><p>TEXTS: Eph 5:18; 1 Tim 5:23</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Church and the public square  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: I value prophetic non-partisanship, political involvement, and cultural engagement. In practice they pull against each other. Work out which governs when they conflict.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Justice and race  [T2.5 · open · study]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>STUDY: Biblical justice is commanded and non-negotiable.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="War and violence  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Just war rather than pacifism. Force is a grim last resort under real constraint.</p><p>TEXTS: Rom 13:4; Matt 5:39</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Formation and practice" POSITION="left" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Sanctification — definitive  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Past tense and accomplished. &quot;You were sanctified&quot; (1 Cor 6:11); &quot;sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all&quot; (Heb 10:10). Set apart, already.</p><p>WHY: This is the language that makes &quot;already sanctified&quot; true without contradicting ongoing growth.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Cor 6:11; Heb 10:10</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Sanctification — progressive  [T2 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Genuine transformation over a lifetime, Spirit-empowered and cooperative, punctuated by crisis moments that do real and lasting work.</p><p>NOT: Wesleyan entire sanctification; and equally the passive &quot;let go and let God&quot; model.</p><p>STUDY: Sharpen the definitions and the relationship between the three tenses.</p><p>TEXTS: 2 Cor 3:18; Gal 5:22-23</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Sanctification — final  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Completed at glorification. Sin remains until then.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 John 3:2; Rom 8:30</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Desert seasons  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Growth often occurs in wilderness seasons rather than in spite of them. Israel in the desert is the pattern. Also note the transitory period of Jonah in the fish and Jesus while buried.</p><p>WHY: Guards against reading dryness as failure or as God&#x27;s absence.</p><p>TEXTS: Deut 8:2-3; Hos 2:14-15</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Hearing God  [T2 · confident · study]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: God guides chiefly through Scripture, renewed thinking, wise counsel and circumstance. Impressions, dreams and prophetic words are genuine but subordinate, and always testable.</p><p>NOT: The &quot;God told me&quot; culture that places a decision beyond challenge.</p><p>STUDY: Same root problem as prophecy — the vocabulary is being made to do too many jobs at once.</p><p>TEXTS: Rom 12:2; John 10:27</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Spiritual disciplines  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Prayer, Scripture, fasting, sabbath and community are ordinary means God actually uses. Grace is opposed to &#x27;earning&#x27;, not &#x27;effort&#x27;.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Tim 4:7-8; Matt 6:16-18</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Suffering and providence  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: God is sovereign over suffering and genuinely good within it. He does not need to have caused an evil in order to redeem it.</p><p>TEXTS: Rom 8:28; Gen 50:20</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Missions and world religions" POSITION="right" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Exclusivity of Christ  [T1 · certain]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Salvation is through Christ alone. There is no other name.</p><p>TEXTS: John 14:6; Acts 4:12</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="The unevangelised  [T3 · open · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Pretty open, linked to the Molinist answer — that God knows who would never have chosen him under any circumstances...</p><p>STUDY: Preferred answer is the Molinist one, not inclusivism. With Molinism held only at T3...</p><p>TEXTS: Matt 11:21-23; Rom 2:14-15</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="World religions  [T2 · confident]" COLOR="#8c6a1f" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Other religions may contain genuine moral insight and real longing, but they do not save.</p><p>TEXTS: Acts 17:22-23; Rom 1:19-20</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Contextualisation  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The gospel translates into every culture and judges every culture — including this one.</p><p>TEXTS: 1 Cor 9:19-23</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Israel and Judaism  [T3 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The Jewish people remain objects of God&#x27;s covenant faithfulness, and need Christ as everyone does.</p><p>STUDY: Study further into Israel-Christian interrelationship</p><p>TEXTS: Rom 11:28-29; Rom 1:16</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Church history and authority" POSITION="left" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="Prima scriptura  [T1.5 · confident]" COLOR="#8a4a24" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Scripture is the final authority; tradition is a real but ministerial authority beneath it.</p><p>NOT: Biblicism that treats church history as mere data; and any account that makes tradition a second source.</p><p>TEXTS: Acts 17:11; 2 Tim 3:16-17</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="The creeds  [T1 · certain]" COLOR="#7c2d3b" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Nicaea and Chalcedon are tier one. To depart from them is to have misread Scripture.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="The Fathers  [T2.5 · confident]" COLOR="#5f6b35" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Weighty and worth heeding — but not &quot;teachers&quot; in an authoritative sense.</p><p>WHY: This is a markedly higher view of the great tradition than Pentecostal practice usually carries.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Roman Catholics and Orthodox  [T3 · leaning · study]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Many individuals within them are genuinely saved, despite rather than because of their churches&#x27; official teaching on justification and authority.</p><p>STUDY: Less confident regarding priests and ministers within those churches. Work out whether holding office changes the assessment.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Reformation solas  [T1.5 · certain]" COLOR="#8a4a24" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Affirmed — grace alone, faith alone, Christ alone, to God&#x27;s glory alone, Scripture first.</p><p>TEXTS: Eph 2:8-9; Rom 3:28</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="Pentecostal heritage  [T3 · confident]" COLOR="#2f6b63" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: The movement&#x27;s recovery of the Spirit&#x27;s present work is a genuine gift to the church. Its thin systematic theology and short historical memory are real costs to be corrected rather than defended.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
<node TEXT="Cross-cutting threads" POSITION="right" FOLDED="true">
<node TEXT="A sacramental instinct" COLOR="#57534e" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Baptism as genuinely instrumental and lean toward real presence at the table — both against low-church Pentecostal defaults.</p><p>STUDY: Key: &#x27;What does a means of grace actually do?&#x27;</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="The vocabulary of hearing God" COLOR="#57534e" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: &quot;Prophecy&quot; and &quot;God told me&quot; are being made to carry several different meanings at once.</p><p>STUDY: Do the word study before settling prophecy, guidance, or the prophetic office.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
<node TEXT="A high view of the great tradition" COLOR="#57534e" STYLE="fork">
<richcontent TYPE="NOTE"><html><head></head><body><p>HOLD: Creeds at tier one, the Fathers weighty, sympathy for classical theism, a sacramental instinct.</p><p>STUDY: Look more into the fathers and how to understand / apply / appreciate the spectrum of views.</p></body></html></richcontent>
</node>
</node>
</node>
</map>